(window.CHAPTERS = window.CHAPTERS || {})["jesus"] = (window.CHAPTERS["jesus"] || []).concat([

/* ===================== CHAPTER 8 ===================== */
{
  id: "j08-all-the-sevens-seventy-seven",
  title: "All the Sevens, Seventy-Seven",
  summary: "Peter asks how often he must forgive a brother. Jesus answers with a hidden hint (a remez) to Lamech’s boast of seventy-sevenfold revenge, then tells the shocking parable of the unforgiving servant.",
  sections: [
    {
      h: "Peter’s question about the “brother”",
      blocks: [
        { t: "p", text: "Matthew 18:21-35 follows straight on from verses 15-20. Both sections deal with sin and repentance, and both speak about **a brother who sins** against another (v. 15, 21). Now Peter asks a follow-up question: how often must I forgive?" },
        { t: "term", word: "adelphos", heb: "ἀδελφός", lang: "Greek", meaning: "“Brother.” In the gospels it means a fellow disciple of either gender — a brother or sister in the new community Jesus is building." },
        { t: "p", text: "Some translations say “someone who sins” to sound inclusive. But the Greek is still *adelphos*. So the question is not about anyone at all. It is about believers we treat as brothers and sisters. There will be enough sins to deal with there!" },
        { t: "term", word: "Inclusio", meaning: "A literary “bookend.” The same word appears at the start and end of a passage to mark its boundaries. Here “brother” opens the passage (v. 21) and closes it (v. 35)." },
        { t: "quote", text: "Lord, how many times shall I forgive my brother or sister who sins against me? Up to seven times?", ref: "Matthew 18:21" }
      ]
    },
    {
      h: "How many times? One, two, three… seven",
      blocks: [
        { t: "p", text: "Peter tries to answer his own question: “How about seven?” This makes sense. Seven was often a symbol of **fullness or completion**. Also, this was a standard question to ask a rabbi. Disciples wanted to know their rabbi’s view, because the answer showed whether he belonged to a strict or a lenient school. Seven may have been the generous answer, and Peter may have been guessing what his merciful rabbi would say." },
        { t: "p", text: "In Luke 17:3-4 Jesus even says to forgive the same brother **seven times in one day** — again perhaps a symbol of being lavish in forgiveness. But that sounds open to abuse. Is the repentance real each time? For this reason some Jewish traditions said **three times** was enough. If someone did not change after a few times, they never would." },
        { t: "quote", text: "If a man said, “I will sin and repent, and sin again and repent,” he will be given no chance to repent. If he said, “I will sin and the Day of Atonement will effect atonement,” then the Day of Atonement effects no atonement.", ref: "Mishnah, Yoma 8:9" },
        { t: "p", text: "The Mishnah adds: sins between a person and God are atoned on the Day of Atonement, but sins against a fellow human are atoned **only if he has first made peace with that person**. Repentance must be genuine, and real reconciliation is needed." },
        { t: "p", text: "The Talmud, commenting on this, says: a person is forgiven the first, second and third time, but **not the fourth**. It supports this from two verses: God works with a man “twice, even three times” (Job 33:29-30), and “For three transgressions of Israel, and for four, I will not turn back my wrath” (Amos 2:6). This way of using Scripture seems odd to us, but it is how the ancient Jewish mind worked." },
        { t: "flow", title: "The rising number of forgiveness", nodes: [
          { h: "Tradition: 3", d: "Talmud: forgiven three times, not the fourth (Job 33, Amos 2)" },
          { h: "Peter: 7", d: "Fullness and completion — a generous rabbi’s answer" },
          { h: "Jesus: 77", d: "A hint to Lamech — forgiveness without limit" }
        ] }
      ]
    },
    {
      h: "Seventy-seven: a hint to Lamech",
      blocks: [
        { t: "p", text: "Jesus answers with **seventy-seven**. Some footnotes offer “seventy times seven” (490), since the Greek can be read that way. A bigger number might seem better! But that misses the clue. Jesus is pointing back to Genesis 4:24, where Lamech boasts to his wives about his revenge:" },
        { t: "quote", text: "If Cain is avenged seven times, then Lamech seventy-seven times.", ref: "Genesis 4:24" },
        { t: "p", text: "“The revenge of Lamech” had become a common saying for revenge far beyond anything reasonable. Jesus picks up Peter’s number seven and answers indirectly but powerfully." },
        { t: "term", word: "Remez", heb: "רֶמֶז", lang: "Hebrew", meaning: "“Hint, clue, allusion.” A rabbinic teaching method: a chosen word or number links the listener’s mind to a passage of Scripture, which then expands the teacher’s message." },
        { t: "p", text: "A rabbi never announced, “Here comes a remez!” The name itself came only in medieval times. Disciples had memorised Scripture from childhood and were listening for such links. If they missed it, the rabbi did not open the scroll for them. His job was to set up a **journey of discovery**. If they missed it — their loss!" },
        { t: "callout", kind: "insight", title: "How a remez feels", text: "Try it with the New Testament: “For God…” “For God so…” “For God so loved…” You are away! But how far — just John 3:16, or verse 17, or the whole chapter? A remez is not a sound bite. A few words trigger a whole passage, adding layers of meaning." },
        { t: "compare", title: "Lamech vs the disciple", cols: [
          { h: "Lamech", items: ["Revenge without limit", "Seventy-seven times greater than Cain’s", "Forgiveness does not exist for him", "Revenge is always on his mind"] },
          { h: "The disciple of Jesus", items: ["Forgiveness without limit", "Not a number to count or a box to tick", "Be Lamech’s polar opposite", "‘Outnumber’ evil with forgiveness, even at its worst"] }
        ] }
      ]
    },
    {
      h: "The parable of the unforgiving servant",
      blocks: [
        { t: "p", text: "Jesus did not invent the parable. He took a familiar Jewish form and used it as a key part of his teaching. Parables were meant to **illuminate and challenge**, and often contain at least one **shock** and one **twist**." },
        { t: "steps", title: "The story (Matthew 18:23-35)", items: [
          { h: "Shock 1: a huge debt", d: "A servant owes 10,000 talents — by one estimate about three lifetimes of earnings. What kind of king lets such a debt build up?" },
          { h: "Shock 2: the king’s order", d: "Sell him, his wife and children. That can never repay the debt — it sounds like Lamech-style revenge on the whole family. (Selling a debtor’s goods and family was actually a common custom then.)" },
          { h: "Mercy", d: "The servant begs for time. The king takes pity and cancels the whole debt." },
          { h: "The tragedy", d: "The forgiven servant leaves free but unchanged. He seizes a fellow servant who owes a small sum (a few weeks’ wages). Verse 29 echoes verse 26 almost word for word — but he refuses and throws him in prison." },
          { h: "Shock 3: the reversal", d: "The king hears, restores the huge debt, and hands the first servant to the jailers until he pays — which he never can." }
        ] },
        { t: "callout", kind: "note", title: "A possible remez to Esther?", text: "Ten thousand talents of silver also appears in Esther 3:9 — the payment Haman offered Xerxes to destroy the Jewish people (about 340 tons). The link is hard to see, so it may be a coincidence. Not every similarity is a remez!" },
        { t: "p", text: "The main point is clear. **We have been forgiven much**, and this should shape how we treat others. No one can sin against us as much as we have sinned against God. Jesus is still answering Peter and backing up his “seventy-seven”." }
      ]
    },
    {
      h: "Hard questions and an alternative ending",
      blocks: [
        { t: "p", text: "If the king is God, does God take Lamech-like revenge? Can he cancel his forgiveness? Does one moment of unforgiveness bring eternal torture? The answer: **parables are not exact parallels** where every detail stands for something. In Luke 18:1-8 God seems to be compared with an unjust judge — but that parable is about persistent prayer, not God’s character. Here the focus is the unnamed servant (it could be you!) and his attitude. The rest is background." },
        { t: "term", word: "apodidōmi (apodo)", heb: "ἀποδίδωμι", lang: "Greek", meaning: "Usually “pay back.” It is suggested it may also carry the sense “cancel, release, forgive.”" },
        { t: "p", text: "Some readers notice an ambiguity in verse 34. The final “he” could refer to the second servant, and the verb might mean “cancel”. Then the ending reads: the first servant stays in prison **until he cancels all that the second servant owed him**. Greek texts read “pay all that was due to him”, which leans this way, though the verb is still “pay”. Whether the Greek really supports this is for experts to decide." },
        { t: "compare", title: "Two ways to read the ending (v. 34)", cols: [
          { h: "Usual reading", items: ["He must repay his huge debt to the king", "He never can, so he is tortured for life", "Often taught as eternal torment"] },
          { h: "Alternative reading", items: ["He must release the small debt owed to him", "This is something he CAN do", "Once he forgives, the door of his self-made prison opens", "About life now — and left open for us to finish"] }
        ] },
        { t: "callout", kind: "insight", title: "An unfinished story", text: "Like much of Jesus’ teaching, the story is left hanging, waiting for us to complete it. Will we forgive the small debts others owe us?" }
      ]
    }
  ],
  extra: [],
  keypoints: [
    "The “brother” (adelphos) is a fellow disciple of either gender; “brother” forms an inclusio around Matthew 18:21-35.",
    "How often to forgive was a standard question for rabbis; tradition (Talmud on Yoma 8:9) said three times, using Job 33:29-30 and Amos 2:6.",
    "The Mishnah teaches repentance must be genuine, and sins against people need reconciliation, not just the Day of Atonement.",
    "Peter’s “seven” suggests fullness; Jesus’ “seventy-seven” is a remez to Lamech’s boast of revenge in Genesis 4:24.",
    "A remez is a hint that calls up a whole passage of Scripture; disciples were expected to catch it themselves.",
    "Disciples should be as extravagant in forgiveness as Lamech was in revenge.",
    "The parable of the unforgiving servant has shocks and twists; parables are not allegories where every detail matches.",
    "An alternative reading of v. 34 makes the ending about releasing the fellow servant’s small debt — the way out of a self-made prison."
  ],
  quiz: [
    { q: "Why does Jesus answer “seventy-seven”?", options: ["To set an exact counting limit", "To recall Lamech’s seventy-sevenfold revenge in Genesis 4:24", "Because 77 was the Talmud’s number", "To quote the book of Esther"], answer: 1, why: "It is a remez to Lamech. Disciples are to be as extravagant in forgiveness as Lamech was in revenge." },
    { q: "According to the Talmudic tradition, how many times is a repeated sin forgiven?", options: ["Once", "Seven times", "Three times, but not the fourth", "Seventy times seven"], answer: 2, why: "The Talmud says forgiven the first, second and third time, but not the fourth, citing Job 33:29-30 and Amos 2:6." },
    { q: "What is an inclusio?", options: ["A repeated word marking the start and end of a passage", "A type of parable", "A Day of Atonement prayer", "A Greek verb form"], answer: 0, why: "“Brother” at verse 21 and verse 35 brackets the passage." },
    { q: "What is the main point of the unforgiving servant parable?", options: ["God takes revenge like Lamech", "Debtors’ families should be sold", "Having been forgiven so much, we must forgive others", "Debts must always be paid in full"], answer: 2, why: "The focus is the servant’s attitude; the rest is background. We have been forgiven far more than anyone owes us." },
    { q: "In the alternative reading of verse 34, how can the servant leave prison?", options: ["By paying 10,000 talents", "By cancelling the small debt his fellow servant owes him", "By selling his family", "By waiting for the Day of Atonement"], answer: 1, why: "If the verb can mean “release”, he is freed once he forgives the smaller debt." }
  ]
},

/* ===================== CHAPTER 9 ===================== */
{
  id: "j09-the-law-is-an-asset",
  title: "The Law is an Asset",
  summary: "In Matthew 5:17-20, “abolish” and “fulfil” are rabbinic terms for interpreting the Law wrongly or rightly. Torah means loving instruction, and Jesus insists there are no “light” commandments in his kingdom.",
  sections: [
    {
      h: "“I have come” — a statement of mission",
      blocks: [
        { t: "p", text: "This begins Part Two, “Sermons and Stories”. We return to the Sermon on the Mount, full of examples of Jesus teaching like a Jewish rabbi. Matthew 5:17-20 deals with one of the most misunderstood parts of Judaism: **the Law**." },
        { t: "p", text: "When Jesus says “I have come” or “I have not come” (v. 17), he is not talking about his birth or a trip to Galilee. This is a **Hebraic way of declaring intent** — a task to do, not a journey made. He is stating a mission aim. And he starts by clearing up what his mission is *not*." },
        { t: "quote", text: "Do not think that I have come to abolish the Law or the Prophets; I have not come to abolish them but to fulfil them.", ref: "Matthew 5:17" }
      ]
    },
    {
      h: "What “abolish” and “fulfil” do NOT mean",
      blocks: [
        { t: "list", title: "Common misunderstandings", items: [
          "**“Fulfil” does not mean “keep every law perfectly.”** No one could keep all 613 commandments. Some apply only to women (e.g. Leviticus 15:19-33), some only to men, some only to priests. Jesus lived a blameless life, yet not every law applied to him.",
          "**“Fulfil” does not mean “complete what is lacking.”** The Law was already perfect (Psalm 19:7). Paul calls it holy, righteous, good and spiritual (Romans 7:12, 14).",
          "**“Abolish” does not mean “cancel.”** Why cancel what the psalmist called his delight and joy (Psalm 1:2; 119:92, 174)? To the Jewish mind, the Law was an asset."
        ] },
        { t: "compare", title: "Western reading vs Hebraic reading", cols: [
          { h: "Common Western reading", items: ["Fulfil = obey every rule perfectly", "Fulfil = finish or replace the Law", "Abolish = cancel the Law"] },
          { h: "Rabbinic meaning", items: ["Fulfil = interpret correctly (and live it out)", "Abolish = misinterpret, so the law cannot be kept as intended", "These are terms from debates over interpretation"] }
        ] },
        { t: "p", text: "In rabbinic debate a typical accusation was, “You are abolishing the Law!” — meaning, “Your interpretation undermines it.” No one can destroy God’s Law, but if you say a law means less than it really does, people can no longer keep it properly. That is like cancelling it." },
        { t: "callout", kind: "insight", title: "A paraphrase of verse 17", text: "“Do not imagine for one moment that I intend to weaken or negate the Law by my interpretations. My purpose is to establish it more firmly. By correctly interpreting it, I will set it on a stronger footing and make it even more lasting.”" }
      ]
    },
    {
      h: "What Torah really means",
      blocks: [
        { t: "term", word: "Torah", heb: "תּוֹרָה", lang: "Hebrew", meaning: "Usually translated “Law”, but its root means guidance, instruction, teaching — giving direction so that we hit the mark." },
        { t: "p", text: "We think of law as a legal system to avoid: break it and get caught, and you are punished. But Torah aims us at a target. **Missing the mark** or **falling short** were ways of describing sin in Judaism and the early church (Romans 3:23)." },
        { t: "p", text: "Torah is more than a list of “thou shalts”. Most of the five books of Moses are **stories**, and they teach too. Torah can stretch to any Scripture that shows God’s will, since stories show the results of right and wrong choices." },
        { t: "steps", title: "Salvation first, then Torah", items: [
          { h: "Covenant with Abraham", d: "God loved his people and bound himself to them." },
          { h: "Rescue from Egypt", d: "God saved them first — they had done nothing to deserve it." },
          { h: "Covenant with Moses", d: "Then he gave the Law, so they could show gratitude and live in line with his will." },
          { h: "Result", d: "God is not mainly a lawgiver but a loving Father guiding his children into the best life." }
        ] },
        { t: "callout", kind: "note", title: "Fulfilling also means living it", text: "Correct teaching was only the start. Conduct had to match. You could “abolish” the Law in two ways: by misinterpreting it, or by interpreting it rightly but not living it out." },
        { t: "p", text: "Jesus also mentions **the Prophets**. This is not mainly about predictions coming true. The prophets were also Torah guides; their messages needed to be understood correctly and allowed to change behaviour." },
        { t: "p", text: "Perhaps Jesus said “Do not think…” because some were already accusing him of abolishing the Law. In the “You have heard… but I tell you” sayings (Matt. 5:21 onwards) he was fulfilling the Law — giving God’s original meaning. He was **not raising the standard**. Inner anger and lust were always forbidden. Torah must change us inside, not just outside." }
      ]
    },
    {
      h: "Light and heavy commandments",
      blocks: [
        { t: "p", text: "Verse 19 speaks of “the least of these commands”. More literally this is a **light commandment** — a well-known rabbinic term. By Jesus’ day the rabbis graded all 613 laws. No law was light in itself, only compared with another; the same law could be heavy compared with a third." },
        { t: "term", word: "Gezerah shavah", heb: "גְּזֵרָה שָׁוָה", lang: "Hebrew", meaning: "“Equal comparison.” A rabbinic method that links two passages because they share the same word or phrase, then studies them side by side." },
        { t: "table", title: "Example: the bird’s nest and honouring parents", head: ["", "Deuteronomy 22:6-7", "Deuteronomy 5:16"], rows: [
          ["Command", "Take the young or eggs, but let the mother bird go", "Honour your father and your mother"],
          ["Shared idea", "A mother", "A mother"],
          ["Shared phrase", "“that it may go well with you and you may have a long life”", "“that it may go well with you and you may have a long life”"],
          ["Rabbis’ ruling", "Light", "Heavy"]
        ] },
        { t: "p", text: "Because both laws end with the same promise, the rabbis linked them and decided the bird’s nest law was light. Taking the mother bird was still wrong, but not as serious." },
        { t: "p", text: "We all grade laws like this — in the Old Testament and even Jesus’ words. But does God? Not according to Jesus, who rebukes those who take “light” laws lightly. A later rabbi, **Yehuda ha-Nasi** (c. AD 135-217), agreed:" },
        { t: "quote", text: "Be as mindful of a light commandment as of a weighty commandment, for you do not know the reward of each commandment.", ref: "Mishnah, Pirke Aboth 2:1" },
        { t: "callout", kind: "insight", title: "A paraphrase of verse 19", text: "“Anyone who breaks one of the so-called light commandments and tells others it doesn’t matter will be called lightweight in the kingdom. But whoever keeps these light commandments and teaches others to do so will be a heavyweight in the kingdom.”" }
      ]
    },
    {
      h: "John got the message",
      blocks: [
        { t: "p", text: "The rest of Matthew 5 shows this in action: murder and anger, even insults, are equally guilty; adultery and lust break the same law." },
        { t: "p", text: "The rabbis called “Do not hate a fellow Israelite in your heart” (Leviticus 19:17) light compared with the heavy “You shall not murder” (Exodus 20:13; Deuteronomy 5:17). Jesus’ disciple John set that grading aside:" },
        { t: "quote", text: "Anyone who hates a brother or sister is a murderer.", ref: "1 John 3:15" },
        { t: "callout", kind: "warn", title: "A question for us", text: "Reading Leviticus 19 or Deuteronomy 22 — or Jesus’ own words — do we rank some as less important? If so, beware: we may be shrinking our own significance in our rabbi’s kingdom." }
      ]
    }
  ],
  keypoints: [
    "“I have come” is a Hebraic statement of mission and intent, not about travel or the incarnation.",
    "In rabbinic debate, “abolish” means to misinterpret the Law; “fulfil” means to interpret it correctly — and live it out.",
    "No one could keep all 613 laws (some were for women, men or priests only), and the Law was already perfect — so “fulfil” cannot mean those things.",
    "Torah means guidance and instruction to help us hit the mark; it includes the stories, and was given after God had already saved his people.",
    "Jesus also “fulfils” the Prophets by teaching their message correctly.",
    "Jesus’ “but I tell you” sayings restore God’s original standard; they do not raise it.",
    "Rabbis graded “light” and “heavy” commands, linking laws by gezerah shavah (e.g. the bird’s nest and honouring parents).",
    "Jesus teaches there are no light laws in his kingdom; John echoes this in 1 John 3:15."
  ],
  quiz: [
    { q: "In rabbinic debate, what did it mean to “abolish” the Law?", options: ["To cancel it officially", "To interpret it wrongly", "To keep it perfectly", "To add new laws"], answer: 1, why: "“You are abolishing the Law” meant your interpretation undermines it, so it can’t be kept as intended." },
    { q: "Why could no one keep all 613 commandments?", options: ["They were written in Greek", "Some laws only applied to women, men or priests", "The Law was imperfect", "They were cancelled by Jesus"], answer: 1, why: "Laws such as those on menstruation (Lev. 15) or priestly duties do not apply to everyone." },
    { q: "What is the root meaning of Torah?", options: ["Punishment", "Legal code", "Guidance or instruction", "Sacrifice"], answer: 2, why: "Torah comes from a word for giving direction — teaching that helps us hit the mark." },
    { q: "Why did the rabbis compare Deuteronomy 22:6-7 with 5:16?", options: ["Both end with the same promise of long life and well-being", "Both mention birds", "Both are about the Sabbath", "Moses commanded it"], answer: 0, why: "This shared phrase allowed a gezerah shavah link; the bird’s nest law was judged light." },
    { q: "Which verse shows John rejecting the light/heavy grading?", options: ["John 3:16", "Psalm 19:7", "Romans 3:23", "1 John 3:15"], answer: 3, why: "“Anyone who hates a brother or sister is a murderer” treats hatred as seriously as murder." }
  ]
},

/* ===================== CHAPTER 10 ===================== */
{
  id: "j10-eye-eye",
  title: "Eye, Eye",
  summary: "Four “eye” sayings in the Sermon on the Mount: the good eye (generosity), eye for an eye (non-retaliation), the plank in the eye (judging), and gouging out the eye (hyperbole about sin).",
  sections: [
    {
      h: "Four eyes in one sermon",
      blocks: [
        { t: "p", text: "The Sermon on the Mount mentions eyes four times — so the chapter title could be twice as long! Hebrew thinking prefers **concrete pictures** for abstract ideas, and the human body often appears in its language." },
        { t: "tree", title: "The four eye sayings", root: "Eyes in Matthew 5–7", children: [
          { h: "Have a good eye", d: "Matthew 6:22-23 — generosity with money" },
          { h: "Eye for an eye", d: "Matthew 5:38-42 — answering injustice without revenge" },
          { h: "A plank in the eye", d: "Matthew 7:3-5 — how (not) to judge" },
          { h: "Gouge out your eye", d: "Matthew 5:29 — vivid hyperbole about sin" }
        ] }
      ]
    },
    {
      h: "Have a good eye! (Matthew 6:22-23)",
      blocks: [
        { t: "p", text: "These verses sit in a passage about money: treasures on earth (v. 19) and God and Mammon (v. 24). Why talk about eyes, light and darkness in the middle? Because **Jesus is still talking about money**." },
        { t: "term", word: "Ayin tovah", heb: "עַיִן טוֹבָה", lang: "Hebrew", meaning: "“A good eye” — an idiom for a generous person who looks out for others’ needs." },
        { t: "term", word: "Ayin ra’ah", heb: "עַיִן רָעָה", lang: "Hebrew", meaning: "“A bad eye” — an idiom for a mean, stingy, self-centred person." },
        { t: "p", text: "The Hebrew *ayin* (eye) is **singular**. Translations often change it to “eyes” or “eyesight”, or swap “good” for “single”, “sound”, “clear” or “healthy”. None of these carries the idiom. The idiom is still used today: someone collecting for charity may shake a tin and say, “Have a good eye!”" },
        { t: "compare", title: "Good eye vs bad eye", cols: [
          { h: "Good eye", items: ["Generous", "Looks out for others’ needs", "Sees God’s abundance", "Lights up your whole life"] },
          { h: "Bad eye", items: ["Stingy, greedy", "Blind to people around", "Sees scarcity and lack", "Fills life with darkness"] }
        ] },
        { t: "list", title: "The idiom elsewhere", items: [
          "**Proverbs 22:9** — “The generous will themselves be blessed” — literally the one with a good eye.",
          "**Proverbs 23:6-7** — the stingy man (bad eye) “is always thinking about the cost”.",
          "**Proverbs 28:22** — the stingy (bad eye) find “poverty awaits them”.",
          "**Matthew 20:15** — literally “Is your eye bad because I am good?” (Greek *ophthalmos sou ponēros*, from *ayin ra’ah*). Here translations rightly say “envious” and “generous”.",
          "**Mishnah, Terumot 4:3** — the House of Hillel: giving 1/40 of produce as the heave offering (*terumah*) is a good eye, 1/60 a bad eye. The House of Shammai set the good eye at an even more generous 1/30!",
          "**Pirke Aboth 5:16** — of four attitudes to giving, two are a bad eye: giving yourself but discouraging others (bad eye towards what is theirs), or wanting others to give but not giving yourself (bad eye towards what is yours).",
          "**Talmud on Baba Bathra 4** — in a debate about sales, Rabbi Aqiba said a seller usually sells “with a good eye”."
        ] },
        { t: "p", text: "In Hebrew the phrase reads “good **of** eye”. The main idea is “good”; “eye” says how. A generous person is good in how he acts on what his eye sees. He sees a good God providing, and so gives more of his resources and himself." },
        { t: "callout", kind: "insight", title: "Why worry comes next", text: "A bad eye focuses on scarcity, which feeds worry: “If I give, there won’t be enough for me.” So right after this, Jesus teaches about worry (Matt. 6:25-34). Worry stifles generosity. The only way to store treasure in heaven is by having a good eye." }
      ]
    },
    {
      h: "Eye for an eye (Matthew 5:38-42)",
      blocks: [
        { t: "p", text: "“Eye for eye, tooth for tooth” (Exodus 21:23-25; Leviticus 24:19-20; Deuteronomy 19:21) was meant well: to stop revenge spiralling out of control. It ended with the second eye. Jesus gives his disciples a different way: “But I tell you, do not resist an evil person.” Four examples follow." },
        { t: "cards", title: "Jesus’ four illustrations", items: [
          { h: "The right cheek (v. 39)", d: "A right-handed person facing you can only hit your right cheek with the back of the hand. In the Roman world, a back-handed slap was how you struck a slave or inferior — an insult and a challenge. Turning the other cheek invites them to strike you as an equal. They may repent… or slap again!" },
          { h: "Tunic and cloak (v. 40)", d: "Men wore a light linen tunic (haluk) and a large woollen cloak (tallit) that carried the tassels (Deut. 22:12) and served as a blanket. A cloak taken in pledge had to be returned by sunset (Exod. 22:26-27; Deut. 24:12-13). Give both and you may be shivering." },
          { h: "The second mile (v. 41)", d: "A Roman soldier could force a non-citizen to carry his pack one mile (a thousand paces). Refusal could mean prison. Jesus says: after the mile, keep walking. Repay exploitation with kindness." },
          { h: "Give and lend (v. 42)", d: "Not a blank cheque. Don’t refuse to lend as a way of getting revenge. Don’t deny someone help you could give." }
        ] },
        { t: "term", word: "Tallit", heb: "טַלִּית", lang: "Hebrew", meaning: "The outer cloak: a large rectangular woollen cloth reaching to the ankles, with tassels on its corners; also used as a blanket at night." },
        { t: "term", word: "Haluk", heb: "חָלוּק", lang: "Hebrew", meaning: "The tunic: a lighter inner robe, usually linen, worn at home and at work." },
        { t: "p", text: "Verse 42 makes more sense with Hebrew. “Ask” can mean ask a question, request, or **ask to borrow**. Hebrew also has different words for two kinds of borrowing: returning the same item (a book, a car) or repaying in kind (milk, money). These form a poetic parallel. English has one word for “borrow”, so translators used “ask” for variety — which gives the wrong impression." }
      ]
    },
    {
      h: "A plank in the eye (Matthew 7:1-5)",
      blocks: [
        { t: "p", text: "There is humour here: a man with a roof beam sticking out of his eye tries to remove a speck of sawdust from someone else’s. “Do not judge” (v. 1) is a **warning, not an absolute ban**. The standard you set will be applied to you (v. 2, a saying older than Jesus)." },
        { t: "steps", title: "Judging rightly", items: [
          { h: "Check the motive", d: "Judge to correct, never to condemn." },
          { h: "Stay within your authority", d: "Only judge where God gives you authority, or you take his place." },
          { h: "Never judge a person’s worth or destiny", d: "Only God can. Words like ‘Raca’ and ‘You fool!’ (Matt. 5:22) are excessive judgements of worth." },
          { h: "Lift up, don’t squash", d: "Judge without being judgemental." }
        ] },
        { t: "list", title: "“Measure for measure” in Jewish writings", items: [
          "“With what measure a man metes it shall be measured to him again.” (Mishnah, Sotah 1:7)",
          "Hillel: “Judge not thy fellow until thou art come to his place” — stand in his shoes. (Pirke Aboth 2:5)",
          "“If the judge said, ‘Take the splinter from between your teeth,’ he would retort, ‘Take the beam from between your eyes.’” (Talmud, Baba Bathra 15b)",
          "Rabbi Tarfon: “I doubt if anyone in this generation is fit to rebuke others. If one says, ‘Remove the mote from between your eyes,’ the reply is, ‘Remove the beam from between your eyes.’”"
        ] },
        { t: "callout", kind: "note", text: "These parallels show Jesus fitted the culture of his day — while adding an extra dimension other rabbis lacked." }
      ]
    },
    {
      h: "Gouge out your eye (Matthew 5:29)",
      blocks: [
        { t: "quote", text: "If your right eye causes you to stumble, gouge it out and throw it away.", ref: "Matthew 5:29" },
        { t: "p", text: "Is Jesus prescribing self-harm? No. Exaggerated language (**hyperbole**) about cutting off body parts was common among both Jewish and Greek teachers on sexual morality. Jesus used a familiar figure of speech, not a new punishment." },
        { t: "callout", kind: "warn", text: "Relief that it is not literal must not make us careless. The vivid picture shows how serious these so-called “lesser sins” are — so we guard ourselves against losing our whole selves in final judgement." }
      ]
    }
  ],
  keypoints: [
    "“Good eye” (ayin tovah) means generous; “bad eye” (ayin ra’ah) means stingy — Matthew 6:22-23 is still about money.",
    "The idiom appears in Proverbs, Matthew 20:15, the Mishnah (Hillel and Shammai on giving) and the Talmud.",
    "A bad eye sees scarcity and breeds worry, which is why Jesus next teaches about worry.",
    "“Eye for an eye” limited revenge; Jesus teaches not to resist an evil person with retaliation.",
    "The right-cheek slap was a back-handed insult; turning the other cheek invites being treated as an equal.",
    "Tunic (haluk), cloak (tallit), the Roman one-mile rule and Hebrew words for borrowing explain verses 40-42.",
    "“Do not judge” is a warning: judge to correct, not condemn — a “measure for measure” principle found in Jewish writings too.",
    "“Gouge out your eye” is common hyperbole showing how seriously to take sin."
  ],
  quiz: [
    { q: "What does it mean to have “a good eye”?", options: ["To have healthy eyesight", "To be generous", "To be morally pure", "To judge well"], answer: 1, why: "Ayin tovah is a Hebrew idiom for generosity — fitting the money context of Matthew 6." },
    { q: "Why is a slap on the right cheek significant?", options: ["It is accidental", "It is from a left-handed person", "It is a back-handed, demeaning blow", "It was legal in the Temple"], answer: 2, why: "A right-hander must use the back of the hand — how one struck a slave or inferior." },
    { q: "Under Roman law, how far could a soldier make a civilian carry his pack?", options: ["One mile", "Two miles", "Seven miles", "As far as he wanted"], answer: 0, why: "One mile (a thousand paces); Jesus says go a second." },
    { q: "According to the Mishnah, the House of Shammai set the “good eye” heave offering at:", options: ["1/60", "1/40", "1/10", "1/30"], answer: 3, why: "Hillel said 1/40; Shammai the even more generous 1/30." },
    { q: "How should “gouge out your eye” be understood?", options: ["As a literal command", "As a new punishment for lust", "As common hyperbole stressing sin’s seriousness", "As a temple rule"], answer: 2, why: "Exaggerated pictures of cutting off limbs were common in teaching about morality." }
  ]
},

/* ===================== CHAPTER 11 ===================== */
{
  id: "j11-the-show-mustnt-go-on",
  title: "The Show Mustn’t Go On",
  summary: "The Beatitudes echo Isaiah and the Psalms; “hypocrites” are stage actors performing their piety; the disciples’ prayer builds on Jewish prayers; and chiasms help unlock sayings like “pearls before pigs”.",
  sections: [
    {
      h: "The Beatitudes (Matthew 5:3-12)",
      blocks: [
        { t: "p", text: "A rabbi’s first lesson often summed up what he would teach. The Beatitudes (from “Blessed”) do this. They also hint at Isaiah and the Psalms, showing continuity between Jesus’ teaching and the Scriptures his hearers knew." },
        { t: "table", title: "Old Testament echoes in the Beatitudes", head: ["Beatitude", "Echo"], rows: [
          ["Poor in spirit (5:3)", "Contrite and lowly in spirit (Isa. 57:15; 66:2)"],
          ["Those who mourn will be comforted (5:4)", "Comfort all who mourn (Isa. 61:2)"],
          ["The meek will inherit the earth (5:5)", "The meek will inherit the land (Ps. 37:11)"],
          ["Hunger and thirst for righteousness (5:6)", "Come, all you who are thirsty (Isa. 55:1-2)"],
          ["Pure in heart will see God (5:8)", "Clean hands and a pure heart (Ps. 24:3-4)"],
          ["“Persecuted” (pursuing) righteousness (5:10)", "You who pursue righteousness (Isa. 51:1)"]
        ] },
        { t: "term", word: "Eretz", heb: "אֶרֶץ", lang: "Hebrew", meaning: "“Land” or “earth.” It can mean the land of Israel or the whole world — Jesus may widen the psalmist’s promise worldwide." },
        { t: "p", text: "The eighth Beatitude (v. 10) is puzzling. Should we seek persecution? The answer: “persecute” has two senses — suffering harm, or **pursuing something relentlessly**. In verse 10 “pursue” fits best, linking to Isaiah 51:1. To “persecute righteousness” is to chase it and not let go." },
        { t: "callout", kind: "insight", title: "Like a sheepdog", text: "A sheepdog “worries” the sheep until they go where the shepherd wants. The dog does the worrying; the sheep are worried! We should pursue righteousness like that — doing the chasing rather than being chased." },
        { t: "compare", title: "Verse 10 vs verse 11", cols: [
          { h: "Verse 10", items: ["“Those who…”", "Pursuing righteousness", "Ends “theirs is the kingdom of heaven”, matching v. 3", "Parallels v. 6 (hunger for righteousness)", "Belongs with verses 3-9"] },
          { h: "Verse 11", items: ["“You…” — those already following", "Being persecuted by others", "Expect it, and feel blessed when it is because of Jesus"] }
        ] }
      ]
    },
    {
      h: "Take off that mask! (Matthew 6:1-18)",
      blocks: [
        { t: "p", text: "“Hypocrite” appears four times in the Sermon (6:2, 5, 16; 7:5) and often in Matthew 23. Today a hypocrite says one thing and does another (“they do not practise what they preach”, Matt. 23:3). But in Jesus’ day it also meant doing religious acts **for show** (“everything they do is done for people to see”, Matt. 23:5)." },
        { t: "term", word: "Hypokritēs", heb: "ὑποκριτής", lang: "Greek", meaning: "An actor. Greek actors wore large masks, swapped them for different roles, and used exaggerated gestures and sounds." },
        { t: "cycle", title: "The three acts of righteousness", center: "Secret, not show", hint: "Tap each act", items: [
          { h: "Giving", d: "Don’t sound a trumpet (6:2). Give in secret." },
          { h: "Praying", d: "Go into your room (6:6). Most homes had no spare room — but the tallit over the head made a private prayer space." },
          { h: "Fasting", d: "Don’t look gloomy for show (6:16-18). Your Father sees in secret." }
        ] },
        { t: "p", text: "Trumpets announced actors on stage. Was a shofar really blown to advertise giving? Perhaps. Or it may refer to the **thirteen trumpet-shaped bronze funnels** on the offering boxes in the Temple’s Court of the Women. Coins clattering down them — “sounding the trumpet” — showed how much you gave. You could make a show by throwing coins noisily (compare Mark 12:41-42)." },
        { t: "compare", title: "Two kinds of reward", cols: [
          { h: "The “actors”", items: ["“Have received their reward in full” — like applause", "Reward from people", "Over once they leave the stage"] },
          { h: "Secret disciples", items: ["“Your Father will reward you” — a ‘will’ of intent, not delay", "Reward from the Father, starting now", "Changes us from within; it lasts"] }
        ] }
      ]
    },
    {
      h: "Teach us to pray (Matthew 6:9-13)",
      blocks: [
        { t: "p", text: "In Luke 11:1 the disciples ask, “Lord, teach us to pray.” They already knew prayers from home and synagogue, but disciples normally asked their rabbi for further guidance. Jesus gave an updated version of prayers they knew — the **Amidah** and especially the **Kaddish**. It is better called “the disciples’ prayer”." },
        { t: "term", word: "Amidah", heb: "עֲמִידָה", lang: "Hebrew", meaning: "“Standing.” A required prayer recited at noon in the Temple, older than Jesus’ time, with an outline very like the Lord’s Prayer." },
        { t: "term", word: "Kaddish", heb: "קַדִּישׁ", lang: "Aramaic", meaning: "“Holy.” A Jewish prayer that sanctifies God’s name and longs for his kingdom; close in content to the disciples’ prayer." },
        { t: "p", text: "The Amidah begins by addressing God as Father in heaven, and “hallowed be your name” matches its third blessing. One main difference: ancient versions say little about forgiveness. Jesus stresses it — and invites us to forgive like the Father. Radical!" },
        { t: "term", word: "Epiousios", heb: "ἐπιούσιος", lang: "Greek", meaning: "The word translated “daily” in “daily bread”. It appears nowhere else in the New Testament or earlier Greek writings — it seems the gospel writers coined it." },
        { t: "steps", title: "Working out “daily bread”", items: [
          { h: "The puzzle", d: "Why “today” AND “daily”? The new word epiousion was invented to express something heard from Jesus; “daily” mostly re-echoes “today”." },
          { h: "Break the word down", d: "Epi (on / upon / for) + ousia (being, substance) — something like “for our being”." },
          { h: "Old Syriac version", d: "This 2nd-century translation, close to Jesus’ language, has lahmo ameno — “perpetual bread”, the bread that never runs out." },
          { h: "Function, not form", d: "Hebrew uses few adjectives. This is not a type of bread but what bread does: it keeps you going day to day — “the bread of our ongoingness”!" },
          { h: "Proverbs 30:8-9", d: "Bread “needful for me”: just enough, neither poverty (tempting to steal) nor riches (tempting to deny God)." }
        ] }
      ]
    },
    {
      h: "Crossing over: chiasms (Matthew 6:2-4; 7:6)",
      blocks: [
        { t: "term", word: "Chiasm (chiasmus)", heb: "Χ", lang: "Greek", meaning: "A reversed pattern A-B-B-A, named after the Greek letter chi (shaped like X). It gives structure and helps memory." },
        { t: "flow", title: "Chiasm in Matthew 6:2-4", nodes: ["A: reward", "B: secret", "B: secret", "A: reward"] },
        { t: "quote", text: "Do not give dogs what is sacred; do not throw your pearls to pigs. If you do, they may trample them under their feet, and turn and tear you to pieces.", ref: "Matthew 7:6" },
        { t: "flow", title: "Chiasm in Matthew 7:6", nodes: ["A: dogs", "B: pigs", "B: pigs trample", "A: dogs tear"] },
        { t: "p", text: "Dogs and pigs were figures of speech for **Gentiles** (compare the Syro-Phoenician woman, Mark 7:24-30). It sounds rude to us but was normal then. **Pearls** stood for wisdom — teaching from the Torah. “Stringing pearls” described how rabbis linked teachings together." },
        { t: "callout", kind: "insight", title: "Meaning for today", text: "Gentiles were not yet ready to keep God’s Torah. In the same way, we cannot force Jesus’ morality on people who are not his disciples. Pigs can’t eat pearls — they just trample them. The result may be disappointment or hostility." },
        { t: "p", text: "In short: when it comes to giving, praying and fasting, **the show mustn’t go on!**" }
      ]
    }
  ],
  keypoints: [
    "The Beatitudes summarise Jesus’ teaching and echo Isaiah and the Psalms.",
    "In Matthew 5:10 “persecuted for righteousness” is best read as “pursuing righteousness” (Isa. 51:1); it belongs with verses 3-9.",
    "A hypokritēs was a masked stage actor; hypocrisy includes doing religious acts for show.",
    "Giving, praying and fasting should be done in secret; the Father’s reward starts now and lasts.",
    "“Sounding the trumpet” may refer to the noisy trumpet-shaped offering funnels in the Temple.",
    "The disciples’ prayer builds on the Amidah and Kaddish but stresses forgiveness.",
    "Epiousios (“daily”) was a new word; Old Syriac, Hebrew “function not form” and Proverbs 30:8 suggest bread that keeps us going, just enough.",
    "Chiasms (A-B-B-A) appear in Matthew 6:2-4 and 7:6; pearls (Torah teaching) should not be forced on those not ready."
  ],
  quiz: [
    { q: "What did the Greek word hypokritēs originally mean?", options: ["A liar", "A stage actor", "A Pharisee", "A tax collector"], answer: 1, why: "It meant an actor who wore a mask in Greek theatre." },
    { q: "In Matthew 5:10, “persecuted because of righteousness” is best understood as:", options: ["Seeking to be hurt", "Pursuing righteousness relentlessly", "Persecuting others", "Avoiding righteousness"], answer: 1, why: "The verb can mean pursue; it links to Isaiah 51:1, “you who pursue righteousness”." },
    { q: "Which Jewish prayers did Jesus’ prayer build on?", options: ["The Shema and Hallel", "The Psalms of Asaph", "The Amidah and the Kaddish", "The Passover Haggadah"], answer: 2, why: "Its outline closely matches the Amidah and Kaddish." },
    { q: "What chiasm appears in Matthew 7:6?", options: ["dogs — pigs — pigs — dogs", "pearls — dogs — pearls — pigs", "secret — reward — reward — secret", "bread — today — today — bread"], answer: 0, why: "Pigs trample; dogs turn and tear — reversing the order: A-B-B-A." },
    { q: "What does the Old Syriac translation make of “daily bread”?", options: ["Unleavened bread", "Temple showbread", "Perpetual bread that never runs out", "Sabbath bread"], answer: 2, why: "Lahmo ameno implies continual, never-ending bread." }
  ]
},

/* ===================== CHAPTER 12 ===================== */
{
  id: "j12-dig-deep",
  title: "Dig Deep",
  summary: "The Sermon on the Mount ends with the parable of the two builders — really two grounds — challenging hearers to become doers. Parables (mashal) were a Jewish form with typical features: unnamed characters, shocks and open endings.",
  sections: [
    {
      h: "Disciples or crowds?",
      blocks: [
        { t: "p", text: "Was Jesus teaching his disciples or the crowds? The crowds were amazed at his authority (Matt. 7:28-29), so they heard him. But a rabbi did not normally preach to huge crowds like a Greek orator." },
        { t: "p", text: "The Sermon was probably not a one-off mega-event. More likely Matthew **compiled** Jesus’ early teaching given in many places in Galilee over time. Jesus sat on the mountainside like a rabbi, and “his disciples came to him” (Matt. 5:1) — the phrase for committing yourself to learn from a rabbi." },
        { t: "callout", kind: "insight", title: "“Came to him”", text: "This expression marks a committed disciple. Jesus’ aim with the crowds was always to turn casual hearers into committed doers who would “come to him”." }
      ]
    },
    {
      h: "The parable of the two grounds (Matthew 7:24-27)",
      blocks: [
        { t: "p", text: "Usually called the wise and foolish builders, it might better be called **the two grounds**. The point is simple: what will you do with what you hear?" },
        { t: "compare", title: "Two builders, same teaching", cols: [
          { h: "Wise builder", items: ["Hears and puts into practice", "Digs down through sandy topsoil to bedrock", "House stands in the storm"] },
          { h: "Foolish builder", items: ["Hears but does nothing", "Builds on the sandy surface", "House falls with a great crash"] }
        ] },
        { t: "p", text: "Sand and rock are not two far-apart places (no beach house!). It is about **depth**: digging through sand to the rock beneath. So the two men may be **neighbours** — like two people side by side in the crowd, or two churchgoers in the same pew. Same teaching; very different results." },
        { t: "p", text: "Having heard Jesus “fulfil the Law” and say “But I tell you”, hearers must now let it change them inside. That is what any Torah is for. It takes hard work — but why take the easy option when disaster will follow?" }
      ]
    },
    {
      h: "What is a parable?",
      blocks: [
        { t: "p", text: "**Jesus did not invent the parable.** Parables are rooted in Jewish culture and Scripture. The clearest Old Testament example is Nathan’s story to David about Uriah (2 Samuel 12:1-4). Jesus based only one parable on an Old Testament text: the Good Samaritan (Leviticus 19:18)." },
        { t: "term", word: "Mashal", heb: "מָשָׁל", lang: "Hebrew", meaning: "“Comparison.” Covers parables, sayings, proverbs, riddles, oracles, fables and similitudes — anything that is not plain speech." },
        { t: "term", word: "Parabolē", heb: "παραβολή", lang: "Greek", meaning: "From paraballō, “to set one thing beside another” — a comparison, usually between God’s kingdom and everyday life. “An earthly story with a heavenly meaning.”" },
        { t: "p", text: "So how many parables did Jesus tell? Answers range from about 30 to 60. Jesus’ replies in Luke 9:57-62 could count as three short, enigmatic parables. Parables can be one sentence, medium length (the two builders), or very short and **paired** (Matt. 13:31-33, 44-46). In Luke 15 three are strung together: the lost sheep and lost coin are “curtain raisers” for the lost son." }
      ]
    },
    {
      h: "Features of a parable",
      blocks: [
        { t: "cards", title: "What to look for", items: [
          { h: "Unnamed characters", d: "So it could be us." },
          { h: "Draws you in", d: "“A man had two sons” (Luke 15:11) rings bells: Cain and Abel, Isaac and Ishmael, Jacob and Esau — this won’t end well!" },
          { h: "Which one are you?", d: "The son lost far away among the pigs, or the son lost at home?" },
          { h: "Open ending", d: "The father pleads with the older son (Luke 15:28-32). Does he come in? You decide." },
          { h: "Illogical behaviour", d: "A father gives part of his estate, while alive, to his most wasteful son." },
          { h: "A shock", d: "Asking for the inheritance now meant “I wish you were dead”. Other shocks: the king’s unpayable debt (Matt. 18), the owner sending his son to murderous tenants (Matt. 21:33-46)." }
        ] },
        { t: "callout", kind: "note", title: "One sharp point", text: "Parables used to be read as allegories, with every detail spiritualised — an ancient commentary on the Good Samaritan is the classic overdone example. Today we look for one main point, usually a sharp one, often found at the moment of shock." }
      ]
    },
    {
      h: "Rabbinic parallels — and one big difference",
      blocks: [
        { t: "p", text: "Other Jewish teachers used parables too, mostly recorded from the end of the first century and more common after AD 70. They share openings (“To what shall I compare…?”, “It is like…”), characters (rulers, servants) and themes (banquets, journeys, business)." },
        { t: "quote", text: "A person who has good deeds and has studied much Torah, what is he like? A man who builds first of stones and then of mud bricks: even much water will not destroy it. But one without good deeds, though he has studied Torah, is like a man who builds first of mud bricks and then of stones: even a little water undermines it.", ref: "Elisha ben Avuyah (c. AD 100)" },
        { t: "quote", text: "A person whose knowledge is greater than his deeds is like a tree with many branches but few roots: the wind uproots it… A person whose deeds are greater than his knowledge is like a tree with few branches but many roots: all the winds cannot move it.", ref: "Eleazar ben Azariah, Pirke Aboth 3:22" },
        { t: "p", text: "Who influenced whom cannot be known — copyright didn’t exist, and parables were spoken before written. But it shows other rabbis also challenged disciples to live what they learnt." },
        { t: "callout", kind: "insight", title: "The big difference", text: "The rabbis spoke of those who study Torah. Jesus spoke of “everyone who hears **these words of mine**” (Matt. 7:24, 26). He placed himself and his teaching at the centre — an astonishing claim to authority." }
      ]
    }
  ],
  keypoints: [
    "The Sermon on the Mount is likely Matthew’s compilation of Jesus’ early Galilean teaching, aimed at disciples who “came to him”, with crowds listening.",
    "The two builders are about depth — digging to bedrock by putting teaching into practice; the builders may even be neighbours.",
    "Jesus did not invent parables; they are rooted in Jewish Scripture (e.g. Nathan’s parable in 2 Samuel 12).",
    "Mashal and parabolē mean comparison and cover many forms, from one sentence to long stories.",
    "Parables have unnamed characters, shocks, illogical behaviour and open endings that invite our response.",
    "Look for one main point rather than reading every detail as allegory.",
    "Rabbis like Elisha ben Avuyah and Eleazar ben Azariah told similar parables — but Jesus put “these words of mine” at the centre."
  ],
  quiz: [
    { q: "What is the real contrast in the parable of the two builders?", options: ["Beach vs mountain location", "Rich vs poor builders", "Shallow vs deep foundations — hearing vs doing", "Old vs new houses"], answer: 2, why: "It is about digging through sand to rock — putting Jesus’ words into practice." },
    { q: "What does the Hebrew word mashal mean?", options: ["Comparison", "Story of kings", "Commandment", "Prophecy"], answer: 0, why: "Mashal means comparison and covers proverbs, riddles, fables and stories." },
    { q: "Which Old Testament story is a clear parable?", options: ["Jonah and the fish", "Nathan’s story to David (2 Samuel 12)", "Lamech’s song", "The Ten Commandments"], answer: 1, why: "Nathan used a story to convict David of his sin with Uriah." },
    { q: "What sets Jesus’ parable apart from the rabbinic parallels?", options: ["It mentions water", "It uses trees", "It centres on “these words of mine”", "It has no shock"], answer: 2, why: "The rabbis spoke of Torah study; Jesus put his own words at the centre." },
    { q: "Why are parable characters usually unnamed?", options: ["Names were forbidden", "So listeners can see themselves in the story", "To save time", "They were all angels"], answer: 1, why: "It draws hearers in: it could be us!" }
  ]
},

/* ===================== CHAPTER 13 ===================== */
{
  id: "j13-dig-deeper",
  title: "Dig Deeper",
  summary: "Matthew 13 continues the strategy: the parable of the four soils sorts hearers into real and casual disciples. Parables both reveal and conceal, and the six further parables use shocks and remez hints to Ezekiel and Genesis.",
  sections: [
    {
      h: "From two grounds to four soils",
      blocks: [
        { t: "p", text: "After the two builders, Jesus’ next step is a similar story with **four** outcomes instead of two: the parable usually called “the sower”. Again crowds gather and Jesus “sits” to teach — first by the lake, then in a boat just offshore (Matt. 13:1-2)." },
        { t: "p", text: "Matthew 13 is the gospel’s central parable chapter. Unlike the Sermon, this may all have happened on one busy day, with Jesus himself linking the parables. His strategy again: will the crowds become disciples?" },
        { t: "callout", kind: "insight", title: "You are the soil", text: "The sower and seed never change. Only the soils vary. So it is the parable of **the four soils**. It is mainly about discipleship — how we receive Jesus’ teaching — not evangelism. Jesus is the sower. You are path, rocky ground, thorns or good soil. Which?" },
        { t: "tree", title: "The four soils (Matthew 13:3-9, 18-23)", root: "One sower, one seed", children: [
          { h: "Path", d: "No fruit" },
          { h: "Rocky ground", d: "No fruit" },
          { h: "Thorns", d: "No fruit" },
          { h: "Good soil", d: "Fruit in varying amounts — 100, 60, 30" }
        ] }
      ]
    },
    {
      h: "No farmer in the story",
      blocks: [
        { t: "p", text: "It is often said Jesus saw a farmer on a hillside and used him as an example. But there were no big professional farmers then, and no farmer could waste so much seed. Instead, people in Galilean villages grew food on **small patches**. Everyone knew what it was like to sow badly! Their first thought: “That’s me!” Then the smile turned to a frown as they realised they were not the sower but the **soil**." },
        { t: "p", text: "The Greek is literally “Behold, went out the one sowing to sow” (*idou, exēlthen ho speirōn tou speirein*) — a Hebrew-style repetition. There is no farmer and not even “seed” in the original. Seed is rightly implied; a farmer is wrongly added." },
        { t: "quote", text: "There are four qualities among those who sit before the wise: a sponge, a funnel, a strainer and a sieve. A sponge soaks up everything; a funnel takes in at one end and lets out at the other; a strainer lets the wine pass and keeps the dregs; a sieve lets out the bran and keeps the fine flour.", ref: "Mishnah, Pirke Aboth 5:18" },
        { t: "cards", title: "Four kinds of learner", items: [
          { h: "Sponge", d: "Soaks up everything, good and bad, without discernment." },
          { h: "Funnel", d: "In one ear, out the other." },
          { h: "Strainer", d: "Keeps only the bad." },
          { h: "Sieve", d: "Keeps only the good — the best!" }
        ] },
        { t: "p", text: "Other sayings followed the same four-way pattern: quick learner/quick doer, quick learner/slow doer, slow learner/quick doer, slow learner/slow doer. Jesus’ hearers recognised the template." }
      ]
    },
    {
      h: "Why speak in parables?",
      blocks: [
        { t: "p", text: "The disciples asked, “Why do you speak to the people in parables?” (Matt. 13:10). Rabbis often used parables, so the question really meant: why **only** parables to the crowds now (Matt. 13:34; Mark 4:34)? Mark says he spoke “as much as they could understand” (Mark 4:33). Matthew quotes Isaiah: they will not hear." },
        { t: "compare", title: "Parables reveal and conceal", cols: [
          { h: "For the crowds", items: ["They get the story", "It stays an earthly story", "Not committed enough to ask"] },
          { h: "For disciples", items: ["Kept “coming to him” (13:10, 36)", "Already dug deep and became good soil", "Given the secrets of the kingdom (13:11)", "They ask, receive more, and act on it"] }
        ] },
        { t: "p", text: "A rabbi did not make everything plain at first. He hid treasure in his words and set up a journey of discovery. This tested commitment and helped growth. If disciples didn’t get it, they could ask. If they didn’t ask — like a missed remez — their loss!" },
        { t: "term", word: "To hear (shema)", heb: "שְׁמַע", lang: "Hebrew", meaning: "In Hebrew, “hear” is close to “heed”: paying attention and being determined to act on what you hear." },
        { t: "callout", kind: "warn", title: "Catering to the highest", text: "We prefer direct teaching: “Tell us straight!” But a parable is like a curve ball you must catch and run with. Many today want church without discipleship, so teaching drifts to the lowest common denominator. Jewish teaching aimed at the highest. “Nice sermon, vicar” — but what changes before next Sunday?" },
        { t: "p", text: "Jesus did not want people not to understand. He recognised that not everyone would do the work. Parables provoke new thoughts and are meant to perplex at first, so disciples discuss them afterwards: “What did he mean? Let’s go and ask!”" }
      ]
    },
    {
      h: "Six more parables",
      blocks: [
        { t: "p", text: "Matthew 13:24-52 is carefully structured. Three parables go to the crowds; then Jesus goes indoors, the disciples come to him (v. 36), and three more are just for them. The first and last match, with two pairs in between." },
        { t: "table", title: "Structure of Matthew 13:24-52", head: ["Parable", "Verses", "Pairing"], rows: [
          ["Weeds and wheat", "24-30, 36-43", "Matches the good and bad fish"],
          ["Mustard seed", "31-32", "Paired with yeast"],
          ["Yeast", "33", "Paired with mustard seed"],
          ["Hidden treasure", "44", "Paired with the pearl"],
          ["Pearl of great price", "45-46", "Paired with treasure"],
          ["Good and bad fish (the net)", "47-50", "Matches weeds and wheat"]
        ] },
        { t: "p", text: "**Weeds and wheat:** the enemy probably sowed “bastard wheat”, which looks like wheat until harvest, when real wheat heads bow while weeds stand upright. The roots tangle together, so uprooting is impossible. Separation is a harvest job for the Son of Man and his angels. Meanwhile we live in a mixed world." },
        { t: "p", text: "**Mustard seed:** the tiny seed becoming a big plant was no surprise. The shock was comparing God’s kingdom to mustard — a **damaging weed** with tangled roots that no one would plant in a field. Laws even restricted planting it beside certain crops. And mustard is a herb or bush, not a tree; even black mustard around Galilee reaches only 3-4 metres." },
        { t: "callout", kind: "insight", title: "Remez: Ezekiel’s cedar", text: "Why call it a tree? To bring in the birds — and a remez! In Ezekiel 17:23 God takes a small sprig and makes it a splendid cedar where birds nest and find shade. The kingdom is God’s work: once begun it is unstoppable and blesses all who come." },
        { t: "p", text: "**Yeast:** yeast usually stands for sin, so this comparison is shocking. But the message is the same: once in, yeast can’t be removed or stopped. It spreads everywhere." },
        { t: "callout", kind: "insight", title: "Remez: Sarah’s hospitality", text: "“Three satas” of flour (Matt. 13:33) equals three seahs — about 22 litres or 30 kg. This recalls Genesis 18:6, when Sarah baked with three seahs for three unexpected visitors. Like the birds in the mustard tree, strangers and outsiders will find a generous welcome in the kingdom." },
        { t: "term", word: "Seah", heb: "סְאָה", lang: "Hebrew", meaning: "A dry measure. Three seahs (Greek three sata) is roughly 22 litres or 30 kg of flour — a huge baking job." },
        { t: "p", text: "**Treasure and pearl:** finding the kingdom brings great joy and is worth giving up everything. (Why was the man digging in someone else’s field in the first place?)" },
        { t: "p", text: "**Good and bad fish:** better named this way to match the weeds and wheat. Again separation comes at the end of the age. We can’t tell good fish from bad and aren’t meant to. The angels sort the catch; we just catch as many as we can." },
        { t: "quote", text: "Have you understood all these things?", ref: "Matthew 13:51" },
        { t: "p", text: "When they said yes, Jesus told them that as teachers of the kingdom they too should bring out **old and new**: remind people of what they know, and add something fresh. Parables are perfect for this." }
      ]
    }
  ],
  keypoints: [
    "The “sower” is really the parable of the four soils — about how disciples receive Jesus’ teaching.",
    "There is no farmer in the Greek; hearers sowed small plots and saw themselves first as sowers, then as soil.",
    "Jesus used a familiar four-part rabbinic template, like the sponge, funnel, strainer and sieve (Pirke Aboth 5:18).",
    "Parables both reveal and conceal: they sift hearers, rewarding those who come and ask.",
    "In Hebrew, to hear means to heed — to act on what is heard.",
    "Matthew 13:24-52 has a careful structure: matching first and last parables with two pairs in between.",
    "The mustard seed and yeast are shocking images; they hint at Ezekiel 17:23 and Genesis 18:6 — a kingdom that grows and welcomes outsiders.",
    "Disciples who understand should bring out old and new treasures in their teaching."
  ],
  quiz: [
    { q: "Why is “the four soils” a better name than “the sower”?", options: ["There were four sowers", "The sower and seed stay the same; only the soils change", "The Greek says soils", "It is about evangelism"], answer: 1, why: "The variable factor is the soil — the hearer’s response." },
    { q: "In the Mishnah’s four learners, which is the best?", options: ["Sponge", "Funnel", "Strainer", "Sieve"], answer: 3, why: "A sieve lets the bran out and keeps the fine flour — keeping only the good." },
    { q: "What was shocking about the mustard seed parable?", options: ["The seed was large", "Mustard was a troublesome weed no one would plant in a field", "Mustard was holy", "It grew underwater"], answer: 1, why: "Mustard spread aggressively; comparing it to the kingdom was unexpected." },
    { q: "The “three seahs” of flour in the yeast parable recall:", options: ["Sarah baking for three visitors (Genesis 18:6)", "Manna in the wilderness", "The Passover bread", "Elijah and the widow"], answer: 0, why: "Sarah used three seahs of flour to welcome unexpected guests — the kingdom welcomes outsiders." },
    { q: "Why did Jesus speak only in parables to the crowds at this point?", options: ["He didn’t want anyone to understand", "Parables reveal truth to those committed enough to dig and ask", "The crowds spoke Greek", "It was the Sabbath"], answer: 1, why: "Parables sift hearers; disciples who came and asked received more." }
  ]
},

/* ===================== CHAPTER 14 ===================== */
{
  id: "j14-the-yokes-on-me",
  title: "The Yoke’s On Me",
  summary: "In Matthew 11:28-30, taking Jesus’ yoke means becoming his disciple. His “rest” hints at Exodus 33:14 and Jeremiah 6:16, and a rabbi’s yoke was his way of interpreting Torah — shown by his choice of the second greatest commandment.",
  sections: [
    {
      h: "Taking the yoke = becoming a disciple",
      blocks: [
        { t: "p", text: "Part Three, “Clues and Hints”, explores more examples of **remez**. Words like “rest”, “weary” and “burden” might suggest a holiday from busy life. That may sometimes help, but it is not Jesus’ point. **“Taking the yoke” meant learning from a rabbi** — becoming a disciple." },
        { t: "quote", text: "Come to me, all you who are weary and burdened, and I will give you rest. Take my yoke upon you and learn from me, for I am gentle and humble in heart, and you will find rest for your souls. For my yoke is easy and my burden is light.", ref: "Matthew 11:28-30" },
        { t: "p", text: "The yoke was a common Jewish image for following God’s laws. Israel’s leaders had “broken off the yoke” (Jeremiah 5:5). “The yoke of the commandments” meant placing yourself under the Law." },
        { t: "term", word: "Bar Mitzvah", heb: "בַּר מִצְוָה", lang: "Hebrew/Aramaic", meaning: "“Son of the commandment.” At 13 a Jewish boy declares himself ready to come under Torah — taking the yoke of lifelong learning. The full ritual developed in the 13th-14th centuries, with roots as early as the 6th; something similar may have existed in Jesus’ day." },
        { t: "callout", kind: "insight", title: "Two oxen ploughing", text: "A young ox was yoked to an experienced one who knew the farmer’s commands. Walking side by side, the young one learned. That is the rabbi-disciple relationship." },
        { t: "list", title: "Clues in the wording", items: [
          "**“Come to me”** — the phrase for a disciple joining a rabbi.",
          "**“Take my yoke… and learn from me”** — a parallelism: yoke = learn. It means “take my yoke in order to learn from me”. (“Learn from me” is better than “learn of me”.)",
          "**“My yoke”** — not just the yoke of the Law in general. Jesus centres it on himself, as in “these words of mine” (Matt. 7:24). His interpretation of the Law IS the Law."
        ] },
        { t: "p", text: "Jesus offers a deeper learning than any book or human teacher: a living relationship, side by side, as we “plough through life together”. We have “one Teacher, the Messiah” (Matt. 23:10). Human teachers help, but make disciples only for Jesus. There is room in the yoke for one Master only." }
      ]
    },
    {
      h: "Heavy yokes and an easy one",
      blocks: [
        { t: "p", text: "All rabbis wanted people to take the yoke of Torah — accepting God’s reign in obedience. But it often became a burden. Jesus criticised **some** Pharisees — not all, for it was a broad movement with many well-meaning members." },
        { t: "quote", text: "They tie up heavy, cumbersome loads and put them on other people’s shoulders, but they themselves are not willing to lift a finger to move them.", ref: "Matthew 23:4" },
        { t: "compare", title: "Two yokes", cols: [
          { h: "Some Pharisees’ yoke", items: ["Heavy: extra laws and wrong interpretations", "Some called “shoulder Pharisees”", "Would not yoke themselves alongside people", "Wearied people gave up and were labelled “sinners”"] },
          { h: "Jesus’ yoke", items: ["“Easy” and “light”", "Still challenging, but less oppressive", "He walks beside us in the yoke", "More fruitful"] }
        ] },
        { t: "term", word: "Sinners", meaning: "In the gospels, often those for whom the Law had become too much, so they stopped trying." }
      ]
    },
    {
      h: "Rest: three hints",
      blocks: [
        { t: "table", title: "The remez behind Jesus’ words", head: ["Jesus says", "Hint to", "What hearers would think"], rows: [
          ["“I will give you rest” (11:28)", "Exodus 33:14 — “My Presence will go with you, and I will give you rest”", "Only God promises this! Jesus does what God does — divinity shown by function, not argued in form."],
          ["“Humble in heart” (11:29)", "Numbers 12:3 — Moses was the humblest man on earth", "Now he compares himself to Moses!"],
          ["“Rest for your souls” (11:29)", "Jeremiah 6:16 — “Ask where the good way is, and walk in it, and you will find rest for your souls”", "At the crossroads, Jesus shows the right path."]
        ] },
        { t: "term", word: "Menuchah", heb: "מְנוּחָה", lang: "Hebrew", meaning: "“Rest” — literally a resting place, like a roosting place where a bird settles for the night. A place to stop “flapping around”." },
        { t: "p", text: "Making all life’s decisions is mentally exhausting. Yoked to Jesus, we let him choose the way, and the stress melts away." },
        { t: "callout", kind: "note", title: "Read past the chapter break", text: "Chapter divisions can mislead. Matthew 12 follows straight on with “Sabbath” — the greatest rest — seven times in English (12:1, 2, 5, 8, 10, 11, 12) and eight in Greek (12:5 is literally “desecrate the Sabbath”). The Pharisees’ extra Sabbath rules were among the heaviest parts of their yoke; Jesus’ teaching is light by comparison." },
        { t: "list", title: "The new yoke in the early church", items: [
          "Peter: “Why do you try to test God by putting on the necks of the Gentiles a yoke that neither we nor our ancestors have been able to bear?” (Acts 15:10)",
          "Paul: “Stand firm, then, and do not let yourselves be burdened again by a yoke of slavery.” (Galatians 5:1)"
        ] }
      ]
    },
    {
      h: "A rabbi’s yoke: the second greatest commandment",
      blocks: [
        { t: "p", text: "A rabbi’s yoke was his way of interpreting Torah, shown by his answers to key questions. The main one: “What is the greatest commandment?” Nearly all said: love God (Deut. 6:5). The **second** greatest defined a rabbi’s yoke. That is why Jesus added a second answer nobody asked for: “Love your neighbour as yourself” (Lev. 19:18; Matt. 22:35-39)." },
        { t: "compare", title: "Hillel and Shammai", cols: [
          { h: "Hillel (and Jesus)", items: ["Second greatest: love your neighbour", "Yoke based on love", "Also kept the Sabbath"] },
          { h: "Shammai", items: ["Second greatest: observe the Sabbath", "Yoke based on obedience", "Also valued loving your neighbour"] }
        ] },
        { t: "p", text: "Your yoke mattered when two commands clashed. Example: **the donkey in the pit**. You must help a neighbour’s fallen donkey (Exod. 23:5; Deut. 22:4) — but not work on the Sabbath. You might expect Shammai to say “wait till tomorrow” and Hillel “rescue it now”. In fact Shammai made an exception, so all agreed — especially the donkey!" },
        { t: "steps", title: "Jesus’ argument for healing on the Sabbath", items: [
          { h: "Agreed point", d: "Everyone rescues an animal on the Sabbath (Matt. 12:11; Luke 14:5)." },
          { h: "How much more", d: "A typical rabbinic argument from lighter to heavier (kal va-chomer)." },
          { h: "Conclusion", d: "A human being is worth far more, so healing on the Sabbath is right. Critics silenced!" }
        ] }
      ]
    },
    {
      h: "Wisdom’s yoke in Ben Sirach",
      blocks: [
        { t: "p", text: "The book of Ecclesiasticus (the Wisdom of Jesus son of Sirach, or Ben Sirach) in the Apocrypha also uses the yoke image. Notice the Hebraic opening “I opened my mouth”:" },
        { t: "quote", text: "I opened my mouth and said, Acquire wisdom for yourselves without money. Put your neck under her yoke and let your souls receive instruction; it is to be found close by.", ref: "Ben Sirach 51:25-26" },
        { t: "quote", text: "Put your feet into her fetters and your neck into her collar… For at last you will find the rest she gives… Her yoke is a golden ornament, and her bonds a purple cord.", ref: "Ben Sirach 6:23-31" },
        { t: "callout", kind: "insight", text: "These are pre-echoes of Jesus’ words: yoke, rest, instruction. But the wisdom we need most is found in his yoke." }
      ]
    }
  ],
  flip: true,
  keypoints: [
    "“Take my yoke” means become my disciple; “come to me” is the language of joining a rabbi.",
    "“Take my yoke… and learn from me” is a parallelism: yoke = learn.",
    "The image is of a young ox yoked beside an experienced one — Jesus walks beside us.",
    "Some Pharisees made the yoke heavy; Jesus’ yoke is easy and light, though still challenging.",
    "“I will give you rest” hints at Exodus 33:14 (God’s promise), “humble” at Moses (Num. 12:3), and “rest for your souls” at Jeremiah 6:16.",
    "Menuchah means a resting or roosting place.",
    "A rabbi’s yoke was defined by his second greatest commandment: Jesus and Hillel chose love of neighbour; Shammai the Sabbath.",
    "Jesus used the agreed “donkey in the pit” ruling and a “how much more” argument to defend Sabbath healing."
  ],
  quiz: [
    { q: "What did “taking the yoke” mean in Jesus’ day?", options: ["Doing farm work", "Taking a holiday", "Becoming a rabbi’s disciple", "Paying Temple tax"], answer: 2, why: "The yoke was a common image for placing yourself under a rabbi’s teaching of Torah." },
    { q: "“I will give you rest” is a direct echo of which verse?", options: ["Exodus 33:14", "Psalm 23:2", "Genesis 2:2", "Isaiah 40:31"], answer: 0, why: "God tells Moses, “My Presence will go with you, and I will give you rest.”" },
    { q: "What did Shammai say was the second greatest commandment?", options: ["Love your neighbour", "Honour your parents", "Observe the Sabbath", "Do not murder"], answer: 2, why: "Shammai’s yoke was based more on obedience; Hillel’s, like Jesus’, on love." },
    { q: "What does the Hebrew menuchah literally suggest?", options: ["A holiday", "A roosting or resting place", "A burden", "A pillow"], answer: 1, why: "It is a resting place, like where a bird settles for the night." },
    { q: "How did Jesus use the “donkey in the pit” ruling?", options: ["To ban Sabbath work", "To argue: how much more should a human be healed on the Sabbath", "To criticise Hillel", "To teach about farming"], answer: 1, why: "If an animal can be rescued on the Sabbath, how much more a person." }
  ]
},

/* ===================== CHAPTER 15 ===================== */
{
  id: "j15-the-green-tree",
  title: "The Green Tree",
  summary: "On the way to the cross Jesus says, “If they do these things to a green tree, what will happen to a dry one?” A remez to Ezekiel 20-21 shows the green tree is the righteous (Jesus) and the dry trees are those facing Rome’s judgement on Jerusalem.",
  sections: [
    {
      h: "A clue spoken under duress",
      blocks: [
        { t: "p", text: "A rabbi’s well-chosen word or phrase, also found in Scripture, made his hearers think further. Sometimes one word was enough, sometimes a phrase. Nearly every Jew knew the Scriptures well enough to catch it — even those outside his circle of disciples." },
        { t: "quote", text: "For if people do these things when the tree is green, what will happen when it is dry?", ref: "Luke 23:31" },
        { t: "p", text: "This verse is found only in Luke. It sounds like a proverb, and its imagery needs explaining." }
      ]
    },
    {
      h: "The remez: Ezekiel 20-21",
      blocks: [
        { t: "p", text: "Ezekiel, one of Jesus’ favourite books for a remez, speaks of trees “both green and dry” (Ezek. 20:47). Chapters 20-21 describe Israel’s rebellion and God’s judgement on Jerusalem and its Temple." },
        { t: "quote", text: "I am about to set fire to you, and it will consume all your trees, both green and dry.", ref: "Ezekiel 20:47" },
        { t: "p", text: "Normally green trees have enough sap to survive a fire that destroys dry ones. But in the fiercest fire, nothing survives." },
        { t: "p", text: "Ezekiel complains, “They are saying of me, ‘Isn’t he just telling parables?’” (Ezek. 20:49). So God gives a **parallel prophecy** — ignore the chapter break and read on! Now fire becomes a sword, and “consume” becomes “cut off” (Ezek. 21:2-5)." },
        { t: "table", title: "Ezekiel’s parallel prophecies", head: ["Ezekiel 20:46-48", "Ezekiel 21:2-5", "Meaning"], rows: [
          ["Fire", "Sword", "Judgement"],
          ["Consume", "Cut off", "Destruction"],
          ["Green trees", "The righteous", "The righteous"],
          ["Dry trees", "The wicked", "The wicked"]
        ] },
        { t: "p", text: "Would hearers make the link? Yes. The story form was memorable, it concerned judgement, and the prophecy had already come true. “Green” meaning supple or living, joined with “tree” or “wood”, appears only a few times in Scripture — so there was little room for confusion." },
        { t: "callout", kind: "note", title: "A warm-up quote", text: "Just before, Jesus quoted Hosea 10:8 in full: “They will say to the mountains, ‘Fall on us!’ and to the hills, ‘Cover us!’” (Luke 23:30). Perhaps this tuned their ears for the hidden reference. (Hosea 10:8 is also echoed later in Revelation 6:16.)" }
      ]
    },
    {
      h: "Who is the green tree?",
      blocks: [
        { t: "p", text: "Translations say “**when** the tree is green”, as if one tree is sometimes green, sometimes dry. The Greek is literally “**in** a green tree”, reflecting a Hebrew idiom meaning “**to do to**”." },
        { t: "callout", kind: "insight", title: "A better translation", text: "“If men do these things to a green tree, what will they do to a dry one?” Compare Matthew 17:12, where Jesus says of John the Baptist that they “have done to him everything they wished”, and that he too will suffer at their hands." },
        { t: "p", text: "So the righteous green tree is **Jesus**. He speaks after being condemned as a traitor to Rome, flogged and mistreated, on the road to crucifixion. Simon of Cyrene is carrying his cross, giving him just enough respite for a short message. A remez lets Jesus say volumes when there is no time to quote all of Ezekiel and preach on it." }
      ]
    },
    {
      h: "Who are the dry trees?",
      blocks: [
        { t: "compare", title: "Two views of the dry tree", cols: [
          { h: "The Romans?", items: ["Pilate condemned a man he called innocent (John 18:38; 19:4, 6)", "So what would happen to Rome in return?", "But the Romans are more likely the “men” doing “these things”"] },
          { h: "The people of Jerusalem (more likely)", items: ["Jesus is speaking to those following him", "They would suffer Rome’s vengeance", "Fulfilled in AD 70: siege of Jerusalem and destruction of the Temple"] }
        ] },
        { t: "p", text: "Jesus turns to the wailing women, who typically followed the condemned, and calls them “**Daughters of Jerusalem**” (Luke 23:28). They should weep for themselves and their children. Naming Jerusalem may also point to Ezekiel’s prophecy, which was about Jerusalem and its sanctuary (Ezek. 21:2)." },
        { t: "p", text: "Days earlier, approaching the city, Jesus had **wept over Jerusalem** — not for himself, but for what would come, because they did not “recognise the time of God’s coming” (Luke 19:41-44). “If you, even you, had only known…” (Luke 19:42). Judgement would come not because they “killed Jesus” (the Romans did that) but because they did not recognise their Messiah, and lost what would have brought them peace." },
        { t: "flow", title: "The argument of Luke 23:31", nodes: [
          { h: "Green tree", d: "Jesus — innocent and righteous" },
          { h: "“These things”", d: "Done to him by the Romans" },
          { h: "Dry trees", d: "Jerusalem’s people, who missed their Messiah" },
          { h: "How much worse", d: "Rome’s vengeance in AD 70 — weep for yourselves" }
        ] }
      ]
    },
    {
      h: "A final messianic hint",
      blocks: [
        { t: "p", text: "“Green Tree” or “Righteous One” had become a **messianic title** — a way to recognise Messiah. Perhaps this remez was a last chance to see what they had missed." },
        { t: "p", text: "Peter seems to have learnt from his rabbi. His letter has a similar “if… how much more” shape, and then quotes Proverbs 11:31 in its Greek (Septuagint) form:" },
        { t: "quote", text: "For it is time for judgement to begin with God’s household; and if it begins with us, what will the outcome be for those who do not obey the gospel of God? And, “If it is hard for the righteous to be saved, what will become of the ungodly and the sinner?”", ref: "1 Peter 4:17-18" },
        { t: "term", word: "Septuagint", meaning: "The ancient Greek translation of the Hebrew Scriptures. New Testament quotes from it can differ slightly from our Old Testament translations." }
      ]
    }
  ],
  keypoints: [
    "Luke 23:31 is a remez to Ezekiel 20:47, where fire consumes trees “both green and dry”.",
    "Ezekiel 21:2-5 is a parallel prophecy (sword, cut off) showing green trees are the righteous and dry trees the wicked.",
    "Jesus first quoted Hosea 10:8, perhaps to tune his hearers’ ears.",
    "The Greek “in a green tree” reflects a Hebrew idiom “to do to”: “If they do this to a green tree…”",
    "The green tree is Jesus; the Romans are those doing “these things”.",
    "The dry trees are Jerusalem’s people, who would suffer Rome’s vengeance in AD 70 for not recognising their Messiah.",
    "“Green Tree” / “Righteous One” was a messianic title — a final hint.",
    "Peter echoes this reasoning in 1 Peter 4:17-18, quoting Proverbs 11:31."
  ],
  quiz: [
    { q: "Which prophet supplies the remez in Luke 23:31?", options: ["Isaiah", "Ezekiel", "Daniel", "Amos"], answer: 1, why: "Ezekiel 20:47 speaks of fire consuming trees both green and dry." },
    { q: "In Ezekiel’s parallel prophecy, the green trees represent:", options: ["The wicked", "The Romans", "The righteous", "The priests"], answer: 2, why: "The parallel in Ezekiel 21 shows green = righteous, dry = wicked." },
    { q: "What Hebrew idiom lies behind the Greek “in a green tree”?", options: ["“To do to”", "“To plant beside”", "“To burn up”", "“To rest under”"], answer: 0, why: "It means “If they do these things to a green tree…”" },
    { q: "Who most likely are the dry trees?", options: ["Pilate alone", "The disciples", "The people of Jerusalem who would suffer in AD 70", "The Philistines"], answer: 2, why: "Jesus spoke to the Daughters of Jerusalem about their coming fate under Rome." },
    { q: "Which verse did Jesus quote just before Luke 23:31?", options: ["Hosea 10:8", "Psalm 22:1", "Isaiah 53:5", "Zechariah 12:10"], answer: 0, why: "“They will say to the mountains, ‘Fall on us!’” (Luke 23:30)." }
  ]
},

/* ===================== CHAPTER 16 ===================== */
{
  id: "j16-the-temple-the-temple-the-temple",
  title: "The Temple, The Temple, The Temple!",
  summary: "“A house of prayer… a den of robbers” is a double remez to Isaiah 56 and Jeremiah 7 — and Jeremiah’s own remez to Shiloh. Jesus warns that the Temple is no lucky charm and judgement is coming.",
  sections: [
    {
      h: "One verse, three clues",
      blocks: [
        { t: "p", text: "This verse appears in all three synoptic gospels (Matt. 21:13; Mark 11:17; Luke 19:46). It holds a **double remez** — and even a clue within a clue." },
        { t: "quote", text: "Is it not written: “My house will be called a house of prayer for all nations”? But you have made it “a den of robbers”.", ref: "Mark 11:17" },
        { t: "p", text: "Setting: the Temple, early in Jesus’ last week in Jerusalem, right after he overturned the tables of the moneychangers and merchants who profited for the priestly authorities at the people’s expense. The crowds “hung on his words” (Luke 19:48) and were “amazed at his teaching” (Mark 11:18). But the chief priests and teachers of the Law “began looking for a way to kill him” (Mark 11:18). Why? Because they heard far more than a few words." },
        { t: "flow", title: "The chain of hints", nodes: [
          { h: "Jesus", d: "“house of prayer… den of robbers”" },
          { h: "Isaiah 56", d: "Temple for all nations" },
          { h: "Jeremiah 7", d: "False trust in the Temple" },
          { h: "Shiloh", d: "1 Samuel 4: sanctuary destroyed" }
        ] }
      ]
    },
    {
      h: "Remez 1: Isaiah 56 — a house for all nations",
      blocks: [
        { t: "p", text: "“It is written,” says Jesus, quoting a snippet of Isaiah 56:7 — enough to bring all of Isaiah 56:1-8 to mind. Only Mark includes “for all nations”, but every hearer could finish the line. Isaiah spoke of salvation reaching foreigners who bind themselves to Israel’s God." },
        { t: "quote", text: "And foreigners who bind themselves to the LORD… these I will bring to my holy mountain and give them joy in my house of prayer… for my house will be called a house of prayer for all nations.", ref: "Isaiah 56:6-7" },
        { t: "p", text: "God wanted everyone, including non-Israelites, to feel at home in his house. The Temple was failing — no longer fit for purpose and bringing God’s name into disrepute." }
      ]
    },
    {
      h: "Remez 2: Jeremiah 7 — “The temple of the LORD!”",
      blocks: [
        { t: "quote", text: "Has this house, which bears my Name, become a den of robbers to you? But I have been watching! declares the LORD.", ref: "Jeremiah 7:11" },
        { t: "p", text: "Jeremiah had stood in the very same place. Perhaps Jesus paused, swept his arm or pointed: “But you…” Just two Hebrew words, “den of robbers”, fired an arrow into his hearers’ minds. He turned Jeremiah’s question into a direct accusation." },
        { t: "p", text: "The dishonest traders were only a symptom. Jeremiah 7 is about **false religion**. The people lived unrighteously yet felt “safe” because they had the Temple." },
        { t: "quote", text: "Do not trust in deceptive words and say, “This is the temple of the LORD, the temple of the LORD, the temple of the LORD!”", ref: "Jeremiah 7:4" },
        { t: "p", text: "The repeated phrase had become a **mantra** — an insurance policy letting them live as they liked, even worshipping other gods. But they could only “live in this place” if they changed their ways (Jer. 7:3, 7). It was God’s house, bearing his Name; he decided whether they kept it, or even the land." },
        { t: "quote", text: "Will you steal and murder, commit adultery and perjury, burn incense to Baal… and then come and stand before me in this house, which bears my Name, and say, “We are safe” — safe to do all these detestable things?", ref: "Jeremiah 7:9-10" }
      ]
    },
    {
      h: "The clue within the clue: Shiloh",
      blocks: [
        { t: "p", text: "Jeremiah 7 continues past verse 11 with a stronger warning — and Jeremiah himself uses a remez:" },
        { t: "quote", text: "Go now to the place in Shiloh where I first made a dwelling for my Name, and see what I did to it because of the wickedness of my people Israel.", ref: "Jeremiah 7:12" },
        { t: "p", text: "**Shiloh** was Israel’s religious capital in the time of the Judges. The tabernacle stood there about 300 years — its first permanent home — with the Ark of the Covenant. Israel gathered there for the main festivals." },
        { t: "timeline", title: "The fall of Shiloh (1 Samuel 2-4)", items: [
          { when: "Eli’s day", h: "Corrupt priests", d: "Eli’s sons Hophni and Phinehas “had no regard for the LORD” (1 Sam. 2:12) and abused their priestly privileges." },
          { when: "Prophecy", h: "A man of God warns", d: "Both sons will die on the same day; Eli’s house will end (1 Sam. 2:13-36)." },
          { when: "Battle 1", h: "Defeat", d: "Philistines kill 4,000. Israel asks, “Why did the LORD bring defeat?” but doesn’t wait for an answer." },
          { when: "The plan", h: "The Ark as lucky charm", d: "They bring the Ark to camp so that “it” will “save us” (1 Sam. 4:3). “The Ark, the Ark, the Ark — we are safe!”?" },
          { when: "Battle 2", h: "Disaster", d: "30,000 die, the Ark is captured, Hophni and Phinehas killed. Eli falls, breaks his neck and dies." },
          { when: "Same day", h: "Ichabod", d: "Phinehas’ wife dies in childbirth, naming her son Ichabod: “The Glory has departed from Israel” (1 Sam. 4:4-22)." },
          { when: "After", h: "Shiloh destroyed", d: "The Philistines seem to have destroyed Shiloh; Psalm 78:56-64 kept the memory alive." }
        ] },
        { t: "term", word: "Ichabod", heb: "אִי־כָבוֹד", lang: "Hebrew", meaning: "“No glory” / “Where is the glory?” — “The Glory has departed from Israel.”" },
        { t: "p", text: "When Jeremiah used Shiloh as a picture of what could happen to Jerusalem, he was seized and threatened with death (Jer. 26:1-9). That shows how powerful the allusion was." }
      ]
    },
    {
      h: "What Jesus’ hearers understood",
      blocks: [
        { t: "callout", kind: "insight", title: "The message", text: "Jesus was declaring judgement on the priesthood for how they ran the Temple. God’s anger would fall on Jerusalem, and its fate would be like Shiloh’s. And through Isaiah: God’s plan to save the nations would go ahead regardless." },
        { t: "p", text: "Linking Isaiah 56:7 and Jeremiah 7:11 is the rabbinic technique **gezerah shavah**: two passages sharing a word can be read together. Both mention “house” — in fact “my house”, at least in the Septuagint of Jeremiah." },
        { t: "compare", title: "False security then and now", cols: [
          { h: "Shiloh", items: ["“The Ark, the Ark, the Ark!”"] },
          { h: "Jeremiah’s day", items: ["“The temple of the LORD” ×3"] },
          { h: "Today?", items: ["“I go to church, go to church, go to church — I’m safe!”"] }
        ] },
        { t: "callout", kind: "warn", text: "Have we ever been tempted to do something ungodly while thinking our religious habits make us safe? Listen to your rabbi hint otherwise." }
      ]
    }
  ],
  keypoints: [
    "“House of prayer… den of robbers” is a double remez to Isaiah 56:7 and Jeremiah 7:11.",
    "Isaiah 56 pictures the Temple welcoming foreigners — a house of prayer for all nations.",
    "Jeremiah 7 condemns trusting the Temple as a safety guarantee while living wickedly.",
    "Jeremiah 7:12 is itself a remez to Shiloh, where the sanctuary was destroyed and the Ark captured (1 Samuel 4).",
    "Eli’s corrupt sons, the Ark as lucky charm and Ichabod (“the Glory has departed”) form the background.",
    "Jesus’ hearers understood: judgement on the priesthood, and Jerusalem would go the way of Shiloh.",
    "The two texts are linked by gezerah shavah through the shared word “(my) house”.",
    "Religious routines are no insurance policy for ungodly living."
  ],
  quiz: [
    { q: "Which two prophets does Jesus hint at in the Temple saying?", options: ["Isaiah and Jeremiah", "Ezekiel and Daniel", "Hosea and Amos", "Micah and Malachi"], answer: 0, why: "“House of prayer” is from Isaiah 56:7; “den of robbers” from Jeremiah 7:11." },
    { q: "What did the people in Jeremiah 7 wrongly trust in?", options: ["Their army", "The Temple as a guarantee of safety", "Egypt", "Their prophets"], answer: 1, why: "They chanted “the temple of the LORD” as if it were an insurance policy." },
    { q: "What happened at Shiloh?", options: ["Solomon built the Temple", "The sanctuary was lost and the Ark captured by the Philistines", "Jesus was born", "Elijah defeated Baal’s prophets"], answer: 1, why: "1 Samuel 4 records Israel’s defeat, the Ark’s capture and the end of Eli’s house." },
    { q: "What does the name Ichabod express?", options: ["God is gracious", "The Glory has departed from Israel", "Son of my right hand", "God has heard"], answer: 1, why: "Phinehas’ wife named her son this after the Ark was captured." },
    { q: "What links Isaiah 56:7 and Jeremiah 7:11 by gezerah shavah?", options: ["The word “robbers”", "The word “nations”", "The shared phrase “my house”", "The word “Shiloh”"], answer: 2, why: "Both speak of God’s house, so they can be compared." }
  ]
},

/* ===================== CHAPTER 17 ===================== */
{
  id: "j17-breakthrough",
  title: "Breakthrough!",
  summary: "Matthew 11:12 seems full of violence. But behind the Greek biazō lies the Hebrew root p-r-ts, “break out”, and a remez to Micah 2:13: the shepherd breaks open the pen and the sheep burst out after their King.",
  sections: [
    {
      h: "A troubling verse",
      blocks: [
        { t: "p", text: "Matthew 11:12 puzzles readers and translators alike. Why would the kingdom of heaven involve violence? Translations disagree:" },
        { t: "table", title: "How translations render Matthew 11:12", head: ["Version", "Wording"], rows: [
          ["Authorised (King James)", "“the kingdom of heaven suffereth violence, and the violent take it by force”"],
          ["New King James", "“suffers violence, and the violent take it by force”"],
          ["RSV / ESV", "“has suffered violence, and men of violence take it by force”"],
          ["NIV (early)", "“has been forcefully advancing, and forceful men lay hold of it”"],
          ["NIV (recent)", "“has been subjected to violence, and violent people have been raiding it” (old wording in a footnote)"]
        ] },
        { t: "p", text: "The words differ, and the verb switches between **passive** (something done to the kingdom) and **active** (the kingdom doing something). We need a clue to break through!" }
      ]
    },
    {
      h: "Some Greek: biazō",
      blocks: [
        { t: "term", word: "Biazō", heb: "βιάζω", lang: "Greek", meaning: "“To use force, inflict violence,” especially a forced entry. In modern Greek also “to hurry” — speed and strength of movement." },
        { t: "p", text: "The verse has two related words: **biazetai** (verb, about the kingdom) and **biastai** (noun, the violent or forceful people). The debate is whether *biazetai* is passive (“suffers violence”) or active (“advances forcefully”)." },
        { t: "p", text: "Some verbs can be **transitive** (needing an object) or **intransitive** (no object needed). *Biazō* is one of these, like *baptizō* (see Luke 12:50). There are also “middle” or “deponent” verbs — passive in form but active in meaning. So the text is ambiguous." },
        { t: "compare", title: "Two readings", cols: [
          { h: "Kingdom suffers violence (passive)", items: ["The biastai attack the kingdom to stop it growing", "Could be religious leaders, Romans, demonic powers", "Or Zealots trying to force the kingdom in", "Or Herod Antipas, who beheaded John — the first victim", "Many say this is the normal sense of biastai"] },
          { h: "Kingdom advances forcefully (active)", items: ["The biastai are bold people zealously seizing it", "John the Baptist and the crowds who responded", "Jesus’ miracles and exorcisms; disciples sent out", "Those “doing violence” to their own sin by repentance", "Evangelists snatching people from evil kingdoms — never physical violence"] }
        ] },
        { t: "quote", text: "Since that time, the good news of the kingdom of God is being preached, and everyone is forcing their way into it.", ref: "Luke 16:16" },
        { t: "p", text: "Luke uses the same *biazetai*, but for people **eagerly rushing into** the kingdom as the good news is preached. There is no physical violence. Luke’s saying sits among loosely connected teachings, without Matthew’s context about John, so it may be a variant or a separate occasion. It helps — but we still need a clue." }
      ]
    },
    {
      h: "Back to Hebrew: p-r-ts, “break out”",
      blocks: [
        { t: "p", text: "In the **Septuagint** (the Greek Old Testament), *biazō* translates the Hebrew **paratz**, from the root p-r-ts." },
        { t: "term", word: "Paratz / Peretz", heb: "פָּרַץ / פֶּרֶץ", lang: "Hebrew", meaning: "“To break out, burst forth, make a breach.” Used of birth, of water bursting a dam, and in shepherding." },
        { t: "p", text: "Genesis 38:29: Tamar gives birth to twins. Zerah’s hand appears, then draws back, and his brother bursts out first. Tamar exclaims, “What a breach you have made!” So he is named **Perez** (Peretz), “breach” or “breaking out”." },
        { t: "quote", text: "The One who breaks open the way [ha-poretz] will go up before them; they will break through [partzu] the gate and go out. Their King will pass through before them, the LORD at their head.", ref: "Micah 2:13" },
        { t: "p", text: "Micah 2 ends with the remnant of Israel returning from exile, gathered by God like sheep in a pen. This is Jesus’ remez." }
      ]
    },
    {
      h: "The shepherd breaks open the pen",
      blocks: [
        { t: "steps", title: "A morning in the sheepfold", items: [
          { h: "Night", d: "The shepherd pens the sheep in a temporary stone enclosure — against a hillside, in a cave, or using old walls — and closes the gap." },
          { h: "Morning: the breaker", d: "He pulls aside some stones to make an opening." },
          { h: "Breakthrough", d: "The sheep can’t wait. They push and shove through the gap, several at once, widening the breach; the shepherd knocks more stones aside with his staff." },
          { h: "Following", d: "Set free, as if “born”, they rush after the shepherd, their redeemer, and follow him all day with him “at their head”." }
        ] },
        { t: "callout", kind: "insight", title: "The real picture", text: "Jesus pictures the kingdom of heaven **bursting forth like sheep from a pen** — a joyful, growing movement. Not suffering violence, not inflicting it. Those who “lay hold of it” experience their own breakthrough into salvation and freedom, and as they surge through like water through a broken dam, they widen the breach and sweep others in. This fits Luke 16:16 too." }
      ]
    },
    {
      h: "John the breaker, Jesus the King",
      blocks: [
        { t: "p", text: "“From the days of John the Baptist until now” shows that **John** made the breach. His radical preaching and baptism of repentance opened a new way, and a movement burst out." },
        { t: "p", text: "But in Micah, the breaker, the King and the LORD at their head seem to be one figure. John cannot be the King — he is in Herod Antipas’ prison. So the King must be **Jesus**. He doesn’t say so directly; he wants his hearers to make the connection and see that what John started, he is continuing as people flock to him." },
        { t: "p", text: "In Matthew 11:14 Jesus calls John the Elijah who was to come — the messenger preparing the way for the Lord (Mal. 3:1; 4:5-6; Isa. 40:3; Matt. 3:3). Rabbinic sources often read Micah 2:13 as **two people**: the breaker is Elijah, and “their King” is the Branch, the Son of David." },
        { t: "quote", text: "The breaker goes up… breaks through thorn fences and prickly hedges to clear the way. Thus it is said of the prophet Elijah that he will come before God’s redemption to turn the hearts of Israel to their Father in heaven… Those returning from exile will break through and pass through the breach as through a gate — they will have courage to turn to God in repentance… Their king passes on before them. He is the King Messiah. He will march at the head of them all.", ref: "Metsudat David on Micah 2:13 (abridged)" },
        { t: "table", title: "Micah 2:13 fulfilled", head: ["Micah 2:13", "Rabbinic reading", "In the gospels"], rows: [
          ["The breaker goes up before them", "Elijah", "John the Baptist (Matt. 11:12, 14)"],
          ["They break through the gate", "Exiles turning to God in repentance", "People pressing into the kingdom (Luke 16:16)"],
          ["Their King passes before them", "King Messiah, Son of David", "Jesus"],
          ["The LORD at their head", "God restoring his presence to Zion", "Jesus — left for hearers to conclude"]
        ] },
        { t: "callout", kind: "note", text: "Hard work on a difficult verse pays off: listening to the Jewish Jesus often brings a breakthrough." }
      ]
    }
  ],
  keypoints: [
    "Matthew 11:12 is translated very differently; the Greek biazetai can be read as passive (suffers violence) or active (advances forcefully).",
    "Biazō can be transitive or intransitive, adding to the ambiguity; Luke 16:16 uses it for people eagerly entering the kingdom.",
    "In the Septuagint, biazō translates the Hebrew paratz — “break out, make a breach”.",
    "Perez (Genesis 38:29) was named for “breaking out” at birth.",
    "Micah 2:13 pictures a breaker opening a sheep pen, the flock bursting out, and their King and the LORD at their head.",
    "Jesus pictures the kingdom bursting forth like sheep — joyful growth, not violence.",
    "John the Baptist is the breaker (Elijah); Jesus is the King, though he lets hearers draw that conclusion.",
    "Rabbinic commentary (Metsudat David) also reads Micah 2:13 as Elijah followed by King Messiah."
  ],
  quiz: [
    { q: "What Hebrew root lies behind the Greek biazō?", options: ["sh-m-‘ (hear)", "p-r-ts (break out)", "t-v (good)", "m-sh-l (compare)"], answer: 1, why: "The Septuagint uses biazō to translate paratz, “break out, breach”." },
    { q: "Which Old Testament verse is Jesus’ remez in Matthew 11:12?", options: ["Micah 2:13", "Genesis 4:24", "Ezekiel 20:47", "Jeremiah 7:11"], answer: 0, why: "Micah 2:13 speaks of the breaker going up and the flock breaking through." },
    { q: "Who was named for “breaking out” at his birth?", options: ["Zerah", "Jacob", "Perez", "Benjamin"], answer: 2, why: "Perez burst out before his twin Zerah (Genesis 38:29)." },
    { q: "In Jesus’ use of Micah 2:13, who is the breaker?", options: ["Herod Antipas", "Peter", "Moses", "John the Baptist"], answer: 3, why: "“From the days of John the Baptist” — John opened the breach, as the Elijah figure." },
    { q: "What picture of the kingdom does the remez give?", options: ["An army besieging a city", "Sheep bursting joyfully out of a pen after their shepherd-king", "A Temple being destroyed", "A storm uprooting trees"], answer: 1, why: "The kingdom breaks out like sheep released from the fold, following their King." }
  ]
}

]);
